:ac_alice_aftercare a :AftercareProtocol ; rdfs:label "Alice Aftercare Protocol"@en ; :aftercareDuration "15-30 minutes after video sessions"@en ; :aftercareActions "Verbal reassurance via video, praise, gentle guidance, release planning"@en ; :aftercareNeed "moderate" ; :description "Remote aftercare for Alice via video call. Max provides verbal reassurance, praise for good behavior, discussion of release timeline. Physical aftercare deferred until Max's return."@en .

:ac_becky_aftercare a :AftercareProtocol ; rdfs:label "Becky Aftercare Protocol"@en ; :aftercareDuration "30-60 minutes after intense sessions"@en ; :aftercareActions "Cuddling, verbal reassurance, water/snacks, gentle touch, processing discussion, wound care if needed"@en ; :aftercareNeed "very high" ; :description "Comprehensive aftercare for Becky following training sessions. Leo and Anne provide cuddling, verbal reassurance, processing of emotional experience. Water and snacks provided. Wound care for flogging/whipping marks. Becky often cries during aftercare - processed as release."@en .

:ac_fiona_aftercare a :AftercareProtocol ; rdfs:label "Fiona Aftercare Protocol"@en ; :aftercareDuration "60+ minutes after extreme sessions"@en ; :aftercareActions "Metal removal (partial), massage, hydration, extended cuddling, processing"@en ; :aftercareNeed "very high" ; :description "Extended aftercare for Fiona following extreme full metal sessions. Partial metal removal for comfort. Massage for sore muscles from heels/restraints. Hydration, extended cuddling. Processing of psychological torment experience."@en .

:ago_alice_collaborative a :AgencyOrientation ; rdfs:label "Collaborative Agency Orientation"@en ; :agencyType "Collaborative" ; :agencyCapacity "0.7" ; :agencyWillingness "0.65" ; :description "Collaborative agency. Asserts needs within relationship framework. Agency is used for negotiation, not opposition. Capacity is good, willingness is moderate."@en .

:ago_anne_assertive a :AgencyOrientation ; rdfs:label "Assertive Agency Orientation"@en ; :agencyType "Assertive" ; :agencyCapacity "0.8" ; :agencyWillingness "0.75" ; :description "Assertive agency. Capable of asserting needs, desires. Agency is used for dominance and self-protection. Willingness is high but tempered by guilt. Agency is strength."@en .

:ago_becky_negotiating a :AgencyOrientation ; rdfs:label "Negotiating Agency Orientation"@en ; :agencyType "Negotiating" ; :agencyCapacity "0.6" ; :agencyWillingness "0.6" ; :description "Agency is expressed through negotiation (shared ownership) not independence. Can assert needs within submissive framework. Agency serves submission, doesn't oppose it."@en .

:ago_beth_low a :AgencyOrientation ; rdfs:label "Low Agency Orientation"@en ; :agencyType "Low/constrained" ; :agencyCapacity "0.4" ; :agencyWillingness "0.3" ; :description "Low agency from economic constraint. Limited ability to assert needs. Agency is constrained by necessity. Growth would involve appropriate assertion."@en .

:ago_bree_high a :AgencyOrientation ; rdfs:label "High Agency Orientation"@en ; :agencyType "High/active" ; :agencyCapacity "0.8" ; :agencyWillingness "0.8" ; :description "Strong sense of agency in professional domain. Takes initiative, pursues stories, makes things happen. Agency is less developed in personal/sexual domain. Growth involves applying agency to personal desires."@en .

:ago_edward_volatile a :AgencyOrientation ; rdfs:label "Volatile Agency Orientation"@en ; :agencyType "Volatile/unpredictable" ; :agencyCapacity "0.5" ; :agencyWillingness "0.5" ; :description "Volatile agency. Sometimes assertive, sometimes passive. Unpredictable. Capacity is moderate, expression is dysfunctional."@en .

:ago_leo_assertive a :AgencyOrientation ; rdfs:label "Assertive Agency Orientation"@en ; :agencyType "Assertive/dominant" ; :agencyCapacity "1.0" ; :agencyWillingness "1.0" ; :description "Absolute agency. Always assertive, never passive. Agency is used to dominate, control, create. No hesitation about exercising agency. This is core strength."@en .

:ago_max_assertive a :AgencyOrientation ; rdfs:label "Assertive Agency Orientation"@en ; :agencyType "Assertive" ; :agencyCapacity "0.85" ; :agencyWillingness "0.85" ; :description "Assertive agency. Confidently asserts control, sets rules. Agency is used for dominance. Capacity and willingness are high."@en .

:ago_nolan_high a :AgencyOrientation ; rdfs:label "High Agency Orientation"@en ; :agencyType "High/active" ; :agencyCapacity "0.95" ; :agencyWillingness "0.95" ; :description "Very high agency. Creates change, takes initiative, makes things happen. Club ownership is expression of agency. Doesn't wait for permission. Agency is directed toward community benefit, not just personal gain."@en .

:ao_alice_moderate a :AutonomyOrientation ; rdfs:label "Moderate Autonomy Orientation"@en ; :autonomyType "Moderate" ; :autonomyCapacity "0.6" ; :autonomyWillingness "0.5" ; :description "Moderate autonomy. Capable of independence but chooses interdependence. Autonomy is less important than relationship. Willingness for autonomy is moderate."@en .

:ao_anne_high a :AutonomyOrientation ; rdfs:label "High Autonomy Orientation"@en ; :autonomyType "High" ; :autonomyCapacity "0.8" ; :autonomyWillingness "0.75" ; :description "High autonomy. Capable of independence, values it. Divorce reinforced self-reliance. Willingness for autonomy is high but tempered by need for Leo's forgiveness. Autonomy is both strength and defense."@en .

:ao_becky_ambivalent a :AutonomyOrientation ; rdfs:label "Ambivalent Autonomy Orientation"@en ; :autonomyType "Ambivalent" ; :autonomyCapacity "0.5" ; :autonomyWillingness "0.4" ; :description "Ambivalent about autonomy. Capable of independence (college) but doesn't value it highly. Autonomy feels lonely, not freeing. Willingness for autonomy is low - prefers belonging."@en .

:ao_beth_low a :AutonomyOrientation ; rdfs:label "Low Autonomy Orientation"@en ; :autonomyType "Low" ; :autonomyCapacity "0.4" ; :autonomyWillingness "0.3" ; :description "Low autonomy. Economic necessity limits autonomy. Capacity is moderate, willingness is low. Autonomy would require economic security."@en .

:ao_bree_high a :AutonomyOrientation ; rdfs:label "High Autonomy Orientation"@en ; :autonomyType "High/independent" ; :autonomyCapacity "0.8" ; :autonomyWillingness "0.8" ; :description "High capacity and willingness for autonomy. Lives independently, has successful career, makes own decisions. Autonomy is source of pride. May conflict with dependency needs and desire for surrender. Growth involves allowing healthy dependency."@en .

:ao_edward_moderate a :AutonomyOrientation ; rdfs:label "Moderate Autonomy Orientation"@en ; :autonomyType "Moderate" ; :autonomyCapacity "0.6" ; :autonomyWillingness "0.5" ; :description "Moderate autonomy. Capable of independence but dependent on Anne for job. Capacity is moderate, willingness is moderate. Autonomy is complicated by need."@en .

:ao_leo_absolute a :AutonomyOrientation ; rdfs:label "Absolute Autonomy Orientation"@en ; :autonomyType "Absolute" ; :autonomyCapacity "1.0" ; :autonomyWillingness "1.0" ; :description "Absolute autonomy. Fully capable and committed to independence. Dependency is weakness. Autonomy is non-negotiable. This is strength but limits partnership."@en .

:ao_max_high a :AutonomyOrientation ; rdfs:label "High Autonomy Orientation"@en ; :autonomyType "High" ; :autonomyCapacity "0.8" ; :autonomyWillingness "0.75" ; :description "High autonomy. Capable of independence, values self-reliance. Autonomy is balanced with commitment. Capacity and willingness are high."@en .

:ao_nolan_high a :AutonomyOrientation ; rdfs:label "High Autonomy Orientation"@en ; :autonomyType "High/independent" ; :autonomyCapacity "0.9" ; :autonomyWillingness "0.9" ; :description "Very high autonomy. Self-directed, independent, doesn't need approval. Club ownership reflects autonomy - created own structure rather than joining existing ones. Autonomy doesn't preclude connection but connection must respect autonomy."@en .

:ap_alice_secure a :AttachmentPattern ; rdfs:label "Secure Attachment Pattern"@en ; :patternAttachment "secure" ; :patternSecurity "0.75" ; :patternAnxiety "0.3" ; :description "Secure attachment style. Trusts Max, feels safe in relationship. Chastity doesn't threaten attachment. Security helps endure denial - trusts Max's intentions. Attachment is strength supporting chastity."@en .

:ap_anne_fearful a :AttachmentPattern ; rdfs:label "Fearful-Avoidant Attachment"@en ; :patternAttachment "fearful-avoidant" ; :patternSecurity "0.3" ; :patternAnxiety "0.7" ; :description "Fearful-avoidant attachment from divorce trauma. Wants connection but fears rejection. Anxious about Leo's feelings, avoidant of full vulnerability. This creates push-pull dynamic. Attachment is core wound needing healing."@en .

:ap_becky_anxious a :AttachmentPattern ; rdfs:label "Anxious Attachment Pattern"@en ; :patternAttachment "anxious" ; :patternSecurity "0.4" ; :patternAnxiety "0.8" ; :description "Anxious attachment style. Needs reassurance of belonging, fears abandonment. Training provides regular reassurance through rituals, check-ins, aftercare. Leo and Anne's consistency helps reduce anxiety over time."@en .

:ap_beth_dismissive a :AttachmentPattern ; rdfs:label "Dismissive Attachment Pattern"@en ; :patternAttachment "dismissive" ; :patternSecurity "0.5" ; :patternAnxiety "0.4" ; :description "Dismissive attachment from economic hardship. Self-reliant, emotionally distant. Intimacy is limited. Attachment serves survival, not connection."@en .

:ap_bree_anxious a :AttachmentPattern ; rdfs:label "Anxious-Preoccupied Attachment"@en ; :patternAttachment "anxious" ; :patternSecurity "0.4" ; :patternAnxiety "0.8" ; :description "Anxious attachment style from conditional parental love. Hypervigilant to signs of rejection or disapproval. Seeks validation from others. May cling to traditional friends despite feeling different. With Nolan, may be hypervigilant to signs of judgment while simultaneously craving his approval."@en .

:ap_edward_fearful a :AttachmentPattern ; rdfs:label "Fearful-Avoidant Attachment"@en ; :patternAttachment "fearful-avoidant" ; :patternSecurity "0.2" ; :patternAnxiety "0.8" ; :description "Fearful-avoidant attachment. Wants connection but fears rejection. High anxiety, low security. This creates unstable relationships. Attachment is core wound."@en .

:ap_leo_dismissive a :AttachmentPattern ; rdfs:label "Dismissive-Avoidant Attachment"@en ; :patternAttachment "dismissive-avoidant" ; :patternSecurity "0.5" ; :patternAnxiety "0.3" ; :description "Dismissive-avoidant attachment from divorce. Self-reliant, emotionally distant, distrustful of dependency. Intimacy is limited. BDSM relationships feel safer because hierarchy is explicit. Growth would involve secure attachment."@en .

:ap_max_secure a :AttachmentPattern ; rdfs:label "Secure Attachment Pattern"@en ; :patternAttachment "secure" ; :patternSecurity "0.8" ; :patternAnxiety "0.2" ; :description "Secure attachment style. Confident in relationship, trusts Alice. Chastity doesn't threaten attachment. Security supports stable keyholding. Attachment is healthy."@en .

:ap_nolan_secure a :AttachmentPattern ; rdfs:label "Secure Attachment Pattern"@en ; :patternAttachment "secure" ; :patternSecurity "0.9" ; :patternAnxiety "0.2" ; :description "Secure attachment style. Comfortable with intimacy and autonomy. Can depend on others and be depended upon. Doesn't panic about rejection. This makes him effective leader and potential partner. With Bree, secure attachment allows patience with her anxiety."@en .

